# yaml-language-server: $schema=../schemas/workflow.schema.json

name: Orchestrate Swarm
description: >-
  Parallel delivery workflow for Beads epics using bounded runSubagent /
  spawn_agent calls instead of persistent live agent chat windows. The
  Orchestrator analyses the epic DAG, selects implementation and reviewer
  agents, dispatches independent work by wave, validates returned results,
  integrates approved branches, and closes the epic when all children are done.

  Execution model: the Orchestrator remains in the primary chat window and
  invokes implementation/reviewer agents with the runtime subagent dispatcher:
  `runSubagent` on VS Code, `Agent` on Claude Code, or
  `multi_agent_v1.spawn_agent` on Codex. Subagents inherit explicit
  session context from the Orchestrator/runtime, verify their assigned issue identity through
  `holly session subagent active --json`, perform one bounded assignment, return a
  structured final report, and exit. There are no separate user-opened chat
  windows, no epic-channel roster, no BEGIN message, and no `holly chat wait` loop.

  Swarm agents and their capability tiers (see swarm-agents.manifest.yaml):
    - swarm-tiny-implementation       tiny_implementation       (<=2 files / <=120 lines)
    - swarm-standard-implementation   standard_implementation   (<=4 files / <=250 lines)
    - swarm-senior-implementation     senior_implementation     (<=8 files / <=600 lines; architecture/cross-cutting, needs senior model)
    - swarm-ui-browser-implementation ui_browser_implementation (frontend / browser-verifiable)
    - swarm-principal-implementation  principal_implementation  (<=12 files / <=900 lines; hardest large-context work, needs Claude Fable 5)
    - swarm-reviewer                  standard_review           (normal review gate)
    - swarm-senior-reviewer           senior_review             (architecture / security / disputes)
    - swarm-principal-reviewer        principal_review          (highest-stakes / senior-reviewer escalations, needs Claude Fable 5)

  Use this workflow only when the epic has child issues that can be described
  completely up front, have clear dependencies, are granular enough for the
  selected implementation agents, and can run in parallel with low file overlap.
  If the work needs persistent negotiation, ongoing reassignment through chat, or
  agents sharing evolving context during implementation, stop and propose a
  lighter alternative such as finer decomposition or sequential `/implement`.

  Composable with the workflow wrapper for universal pre/postflight.

preflight:
  - prompt: >-
      Mode gate — single-agent vs multi-agent.

      Swarm delivery requires `multi-agent` mode. Run `holly mode` (or read it
      from `holly status`). If it reports `single-agent`, STOP: do NOT dispatch
      any swarm workers — the PreToolUse hook hard-blocks
      `Agent`/`Task`/`runSubagent` and the run will fail. Either deliver the epic
      directly in the Orchestrator session, or ask the user to switch with
      `holly mode multi` (this session) or `holly mode multi --default` (project
      default). Only continue this workflow when the mode is `multi-agent`.
    category: discovery

  - prompt: >-
      Load and verify the epic:

      1. The user MUST provide a Beads epic ID. If not, ask for one.
      2. Run `holly tracker show <epic-id> --json` and verify the bead is type `epic`.
         If it is not an epic, STOP and explain that orchestrate-swarm requires
         a pre-existing epic with child issues.
      3. Run `holly tracker exec -- list --parent <epic-id> --json` and record every child ID,
         title, status, priority, dependency, label, and parent relationship.
      4. If the epic has zero children, STOP and suggest `/track <description>`
         or `/track <epic-id>` to decompose the work first.
      5. Record: epic_id, epic_title, child_count, and child_list.
    category: discovery

  - prompt: >-
      Run the formula and molecule gates before any manual DAG reasoning:

      1. Run `holly tracker exec -- formula list` and record formula_list_checked: true.
      2. Run `holly tracker exec -- mol show <epic-id>` and record molecule_checked: true.
      3. If molecule metadata exists, record molecule_metadata_present: true
         and use any stored wave, agent-path, complexity, and file-scope
         metadata as the primary planning input.
      4. If no molecule metadata exists, record molecule_metadata_present:
         false and continue to explicit DAG validation.
      5. Do not infer missing formula or molecule results.
    category: discovery
    requires:
      - epic_id from preflight step 1

  - prompt: >-
      Validate the dependency DAG and swarmability:

      1. Run `holly tracker exec -- dep tree <epic-id>` to inspect child dependencies.
      2. Run `holly tracker exec -- swarm validate <epic-id>` if available. If unavailable, fall
         back to manual topological sorting from the child dependency graph.
      3. Determine wave labels, agent-path labels, merge points, blocked
         children, and maximum useful parallelism.
      4. If child issues lack wave-N or agent-N labels, apply them with
         `holly tracker exec -- label add <child-id> wave-N` and `holly tracker exec -- label add <child-id> agent-N`
         or `holly tracker exec -- label add <child-id> agent-shared` as appropriate. Add one
         label per command.
      5. Re-check that every child has exactly one wave-N label and exactly one
         agent-N or agent-shared label before proceeding.
    category: discovery
    requires:
      - child_list from preflight step 1
      - formula_list_checked from preflight step 2
      - molecule_checked from preflight step 2

  - prompt: >-
      Decide whether runtime subagent swarm delivery is suitable:

      1. Inspect each agent path for file overlap, shared contracts, data
         migrations, schema/API boundary changes, authentication or permission
         changes, and user-facing UI risk.
      2. Confirm each launchable child has explicit acceptance criteria,
         allowed files or expected file areas, verification commands, and a
         clear branch/worktree ownership model.
      3. STOP if the work needs persistent chat coordination, live negotiation
         between agents, unresolved cross-track contracts, or continuous idle
         reassignment. Present lighter alternatives instead: split the work more
         finely, run sequential `/implement` delivery, or ask the user whether
         to defer.
      4. Proceed only if each wave can be dispatched as bounded assignments
         where subagents return final reports instead of waiting in chat.
      5. Record a suitability decision with: suitable true/false, reasons,
         max_parallelism, file-overlap risks, and any forced sequential gates.
    category: discovery
    requires:
      - swarmability assessment from preflight step 3

  - prompt: >-
      Resolve the runtime dispatch surface before launch planning:

      1. If running in VS Code, use bounded `runSubagent` calls.
      2. If running in Claude Code, use bounded `Agent` calls.
      3. If running in Codex, use `multi_agent_v1.spawn_agent` with
         `agent_type: "worker"`. Codex workers do not automatically load Holly
         `.agent.md` personas, so every Codex worker prompt must include the
         selected swarm role, issue ID, worktree/session expectations, allowed
         files or file areas, required skills, verification commands, and the
         rules that workers must not close issues, merge branches, or revert
         unrelated changes.
      4. For Codex model overrides use exact tool names:
         `gpt-5.3-codex-spark` for tiny/light envelopes, `gpt-5.4` for
         standard envelopes, and `gpt-5.5` for senior envelopes. Treat
         `holly:track:model-spark` and `holly:track:model-gpt-5-5` labels as
         routing hints; still run the granularity gate.
      5. Record: runtime_dispatcher, model_override_available true/false, and
         model_identity_audit. For Codex, the accepted spawn call proves only
         the requested model override; workers cannot independently report an
         `actual_model`.
    category: discovery
    requires:
      - suitability decision from preflight step 4

  - prompt: >-
      Run the granularity gate before any subagent launch planning:

      1. Inspect every open child issue and classify the smallest safe
         implementation agent it can be assigned to:
         - `swarm-tiny-implementation`: one tiny mechanical change, one clear
           behaviour or cleanup, usually 1-2 files, no cross-module contracts,
           no user-facing judgement, one focused verification command.
         - `swarm-standard-implementation`: one focused bug fix or
           implementation slice, usually 1-4 files, clear acceptance criteria,
           no architecture, migration, auth, security, broad refactor, or
           ambiguous product judgement.
         - `swarm-senior-implementation`: one coherent architecture-bearing
           change with explicit scope — cross-cutting refactor, migration,
           concurrency, or security-sensitive work the cheaper tiers must
           reject. Up to ~8 files / 600 lines, still one issue. Requires a
           senior model; escalate if none is available.
         - `swarm-ui-browser-implementation`: one scoped UI/browser-flow slice
           with a known route/component, expected states, accessibility
           expectations, and browser-verifiable acceptance criteria; no broad
           redesign or design-system architecture.
         - direct orchestration: anything that is not one coherent issue even
           at the senior tier — unsettled cross-track contracts, ambiguous
           product decisions, or work that must be split before delivery.

      2. For each child, verify all of these dispatch prerequisites exist:
         - acceptance criteria specific enough to test,
         - expected file areas or allowed files,
         - forbidden files or explicit scope boundaries,
         - dependency status and required base branch,
         - verification commands or visual evidence requirements,
         - clear rollback or follow-up handling for out-of-scope discoveries.

      3. Mark a child oversized if any of the following are true:
         - it contains multiple independent behaviours,
         - it spans unrelated subsystems,
         - expected edits exceed the selected agent envelope
           (tiny: ~2 files/120 lines; standard and ui-browser: ~4 files/250 lines),
         - verification requires multiple unrelated suites or manual judgement,
         - dependencies are unclear or cross-track contracts are unsettled,
         - success cannot be checked from a bounded final report.

      4. For every oversized child, propose a split into smaller replacement
         children under the same parent epic. Each proposed child MUST have a
         concise action title, one deliverable behaviour, acceptance criteria,
         expected allowed and forbidden files, dependencies, wave-N and
         agent-N/agent-shared label recommendations, the cheapest suitable
         implementation agent, a reviewer recommendation, and a verification
         command or evidence requirement.

      5. Present the split plan to the user and wait for explicit approval
         before changing Beads. Do NOT silently rewrite the epic.

      6. If the user approves splitting, create the replacement children with
         `holly tracker create`, wire dependencies with stable adapter commands, apply one
         label per command, mark the oversized original as superseded or
         deferred with a reason that lists the replacement child IDs, then
         reload the child list and rerun DAG validation.

      7. If the user declines splitting, do not dispatch the oversized child to a
         cheap implementation agent. Route it to direct Orchestrator handling, a
         stronger one-shot agent, sequential `/implement` delivery, or defer it.

      8. Record: granularity_checked: true, granularity_ready: true | false,
         oversized_children, split_plan_required: true | false, agent_envelope
         decisions, and any children forced out of cheap-agent dispatch.
    category: discovery
    requires:
      - suitability decision from preflight step 4
      - runtime dispatch surface from preflight step 5

  - prompt: >-
      Present the swarm preflight report and wait for user confirmation before
      launching implementation subagents.

      PRECONDITION: The granularity gate from preflight step 6 MUST have been
      completed. If granularity_checked is not true, STOP and complete that gate
      before presenting this report.

      The report MUST include:

        epic_id: <id>
        epic_title: <title>
        child_count: <N>
        formula_list_checked: true
        molecule_checked: true
        molecule_metadata_present: true | false
        granularity_checked: true
        granularity_ready: true | false
        oversized_children: <list or none>
        split_plan_required: true | false
        waves: <wave summary>
        agent_paths: <agent path summary>
        max_parallelism: <N>
        runtime_dispatcher: runSubagent | Agent | spawn_agent
        model_identity_audit: <what can and cannot be proven>
        runtime_subagent_suitable: true | false
        reason_for_suitability_decision: <text>
        forced_sequential_gates: <list or none>
        file_overlap_risks: <list or none>

      If runtime_subagent_suitable is false or granularity_ready is false, STOP
      after presenting the report. If split_plan_required is true, present the
      split plan and wait for explicit user approval before creating or changing
      any Beads issues.
    category: discovery
    requires:
      - suitability decision from preflight step 4
      - runtime dispatch surface from preflight step 5
      - granularity gate from preflight step 6

steps:
  - prompt: >-
      Build the wave-by-wave swarm delivery plan:

      1. Confirm granularity_checked is true and granularity_ready is true. If
         either is false, STOP and return to the granularity gate. Do not launch
         cheap implementation agents with oversized work.
      2. Group children by wave-N label. Within each wave, group by agent-N path
         and identify any agent-shared integration/review children.
      3. For every implementation child, choose the cheapest custom
         implementation agent whose capability envelope fits the task:
         - `swarm-tiny-implementation` for tiny, mechanical, low-risk edits.
         - `swarm-standard-implementation` for focused small to medium impl.
         - `swarm-senior-implementation` for architecture-bearing, cross-cutting,
           migration, concurrency, or security-sensitive implementation with
           explicit scope (requires a senior model; escalate if unavailable).
         - `swarm-ui-browser-implementation` for frontend, browser-flow, UI,
           accessibility, and browser-verifiable work.
      4. For every review gate, choose:
         - `swarm-reviewer` for normal scoped implementation review.
         - `swarm-senior-reviewer` for high-risk, disputed, architectural,
           security-sensitive, data-sensitive, migration, or cross-cutting work.
      5. Keep useful parallelism conservative: do not dispatch two subagents
         that are expected to edit the same file unless one is read-only review
         or the dependency plan explicitly serialises integration.
      6. Default reviewer capacity to ceil(worker_count / 2), minimum 1, when
         multiple implementation workers will return in the same wave.
      7. Present a launch plan with child ID, assigned custom agent, reviewer,
         expected files, verification commands, base branch, and dependencies.
    category: discovery
    requires:
      - confirmed swarm preflight report
      - granularity gate from preflight step 6

  - prompt: >-
      Run the dispatch preflight gate, then prepare self-contained runtime
      subagent prompts for the current wave.

      DISPATCH PREFLIGHT GATE (model + tool routing) — for every child about to
      be launched, determine and record BEFORE building its prompt:

        1. requested_capability_tier: from the granularity classification and
           swarm-agents.manifest.yaml.
        2. available_runtime: the runtime the subagent will run in.
        3. available_model_strength: resolve via model-capability-tiers.yaml.
        4. required_tools: include whether the task needs the browser tool.
        5. fallback_decision: if the preferred model is unavailable, choose the
           strongest available model that still meets minimum_model_strength.
           Record fallback_used true/false.
        6. escalation_decision: if no available model meets the minimum tier,
           DO NOT dispatch. For senior_review specifically (fallback_allowed is
           false), escalate to the user. For a ui_browser child that needs
           visual proof when no browser tool is available, escalate instead of
           dispatching.

      Do not dispatch any child whose required tier cannot be satisfied. Record
      the per-child routing decision in the launch plan.

      Each implementation prompt MUST include:

      1. ASSIGNMENT HEADER: assigned issue ID, parent epic ID, assigned custom
         agent name, wave number, dependency status.

      2. SESSION BOOTSTRAP CONTRACT:
         - This is a bounded runtime subagent invocation inside the explicit
           session context inherited from the Orchestrator/runtime. In Codex this means
           `multi_agent_v1.spawn_agent(agent_type="worker")`; in VS Code it is
           `runSubagent`; in Claude Code it is `Agent`.
         - Do NOT run `holly session get`, do NOT create a new session, and do
           NOT manually call `holly session subagent register`.
         - Stop if `HOLLY_SESSION_ID` is unset; it must be inherited from the
           Orchestrator/runtime and treated as read-only.
         - If any `holly ...` command is routed through context-mode, prefix it
           as `HOLLY_SESSION_ID=$HOLLY_SESSION_ID holly ...` before deciding the
           inherited session is missing. If that expands empty inside
           context-mode, pass the existing parent-shell value explicitly.
         - First run: `holly task status`.
         - Then run: `holly session subagent active --json`.
         - Find the active subagent entry for the assigned issue ID. Ignore
           unrelated active subagents. If the assigned issue ID is missing,
           STOP and report the bootstrap failure.
         - On completion, run exactly:
           `holly session subagent complete <ISSUE_ID> --status <status>`.
         - PARENT/CHILD ISOLATION: NEVER run `holly task start` or
           `holly task finish` under the inherited Orchestrator `HOLLY_SESSION_ID`
           — it overwrites the Orchestrator's `current_task`/`worktree_path`
           (TIZA-873 regression). The SubagentStart hook prints your own CHILD
           session id (`export HOLLY_SESSION_ID=<child-id>`); any task lifecycle
           you run uses THAT child id, never the parent id. `holly task start`
           refuses to run on a session that parents active subagents, so a leaked
           parent id is blocked before it can corrupt parent state.

      3. WORKTREE AND GIT CONTRACT:
         - The normal flow is push-and-stop: create your worktree, implement,
           push the task branch, and STOP — the Orchestrator integrates and
           closes. Do NOT run `holly task finish` and do NOT merge to main.
         - If you create the worktree through the Holly task lifecycle, run it
           under your CHILD session id, never the inherited parent id:
           `export HOLLY_SESSION_ID=<child-id>` (the value SubagentStart printed)
           then
           `HOLLY_SESSION_ID=<child-id> holly task start --id <ISSUE_ID> --intent 'Implement <ISSUE_ID>'`.
           If SubagentStart did not print a child id, do NOT run task start —
           create the worktree via the adapter/`holly worktree create` and report.
         - Work only inside that task worktree.
         - Commit with the issue ID in the commit message.
         - Push the task branch when implementation is ready for review.

      4. SCOPE CONTRACT: issue description, acceptance criteria, allowed files or
         expected file areas, forbidden files, dependency context, and exact
         verification commands. Tell the subagent to reject or escalate if the
         task is broader than its agent definition permits.

      5. RETURN CONTRACT — the final answer is a handoff and MUST contain:
         - status, issue_id, branch_name, worktree_path, the EXACT commit_sha
           (full SHA, not a branch name), files_changed, the exact verification
           commands run and their pass/fail results, the evidence path or URL
           (screenshots/video/logs/uploaded attachments named explicitly), and the
           release/handoff status (pushed? released? resulting issue state),
         - reviewer recommendation (normal | senior), blockers, risks, follow-up
           suggestions, AND the model+runtime report fields (runtime_used,
           model_used, capability_tier_requested, capability_tier_satisfied,
           fallback_used, fallback_limitations, escalation_required).
         - EVIDENCE: a Storybook component capture is acceptable proof only for an
           isolated component/styling change; when acceptance depends on the
           component inside a real authenticated route, authenticated-route proof
           is REQUIRED — state which you captured and why it satisfies the
           acceptance criteria.

      No implementation prompt may include `holly chat wait`, BEGIN/SIGN-OFF, or live
      channel registration instructions.
    category: delivery
    requires:
      - launch plan from step 1

  - prompt: >-
      Dispatch implementation subagents for the current wave:

      1. Confirm all dependencies for the wave are closed, approved, or
         explicitly deferred before launch.
      2. For each independent child in the wave whose dispatch preflight gate
         passed, call runSubagent with the selected custom implementation agent
         and the prepared prompt. When tool support allows it, dispatch
         independent calls in parallel in the same turn.
      3. Do not launch children from later waves until all blocking children in
         the current wave have returned, passed review, and been integrated or
         explicitly deferred.
      4. If a subagent returns blocked or failed, triage the blocker. Retry once
         with clearer instructions if the issue is prompt/scoping ambiguity;
         otherwise escalate to a stronger implementation agent or ask the user.
      5. Record each returned branch, worktree path, commit SHA, verification
         result, changed-file list, residual risk, and the model+runtime report.
    category: delivery
    requires:
      - prepared runtime subagent prompts from step 2

  - prompt: >-
      Dispatch reviewer subagents for returned implementation work:

      1. For each successful implementation return, prepare a reviewer prompt
         with the child issue ID, parent epic ID, implementation branch,
         worktree path, commit SHA, changed files, acceptance criteria, allowed
         and forbidden scope, and verification evidence.
      2. Include the same inherited-session bootstrap contract used for
         implementation subagents. Reviewers also complete their active subagent
         entry with targeted `holly session subagent complete <ISSUE_ID>`.
      3. Run the reviewer dispatch preflight gate first. Call runSubagent with
         `swarm-reviewer` for normal review or `swarm-senior-reviewer` for
         senior review triggers. If senior review is required and no
         senior-capable model is available, escalate to the user rather than
         approving on an under-powered model.
      4. Require reviewer output to contain: verdict approve | changes-requested
         | blocked | escalate, blocking findings, non-blocking findings, tests
         checked, scope assessment, closure recommendation, and the
         model+runtime report fields.
      5. If review requests changes, send one bounded fix prompt back to the
         original implementation agent when safe. Escalate to senior review or
         the user if the fix would change architecture, scope, or dependencies.
    category: quality
    requires:
      - implementation returns from step 3

  - prompt: >-
      Integrate approved wave branches:

      1. Use a dedicated integration worktree for the parent epic or the current
         orchestration issue. Do not merge from the main checkout.
      2. Pull the latest origin/main before each integration batch.
      3. Merge approved branches one at a time in dependency order. If conflicts
         occur, stop and resolve deliberately; do not let unrelated local edits
         leak into the merge.
      4. After each merge, run the verification commands relevant to the merged
         child. After the full wave merge, run the wave-level quality gates.
      5. Close each child only after its branch is merged, verification passes,
         and the reviewer verdict is approve. Only the Orchestrator closes
         issues; swarm agents never do.
      6. Push the integrated branch or main branch according to the active repo
         policy, then record the landed commit SHA for each child.
    category: delivery
    requires:
      - approved reviewer verdicts from step 4

  - prompt: >-
      Advance waves until the epic is complete:

      1. Recompute ready children after each integrated wave.
      2. Repeat the dispatch preflight gate, prompt preparation, implementation
         dispatch, review dispatch, and integration for the next unblocked wave.
      3. Do not skip agent-shared integration children; run them after their
         dependencies and before dependent waves.
      4. If useful parallelism drops below 2 or the remaining work is a shared
         integration path, execute directly or with a single subagent rather
         than pretending it is still a swarm.
      5. File follow-up Beads issues for out-of-scope discoveries before closing
         the epic.
    category: delivery
    requires:
      - integrated current wave from step 5

postflight:
  - prompt: >-
      Finalise the swarm delivery:

      1. Verify every child is closed, deferred with an explicit reason, or
         captured as a follow-up issue.
      2. Run final project quality gates appropriate to the changed files.
      3. Close the parent epic only after all required children are closed or
         explicitly deferred.
      4. Sync tracker state according to the active adapter: run
         `holly tracker sync` for Beads/Dolt projects; skip it for Paperclip
         because Paperclip sync is a live-API no-op.
      5. Use `eval "$(holly task finish)"` to land completed task work; verify
         remaining task state with `holly task status`.
      6. Clean up completed task workspaces when safe.
      7. Record a short orchestration lesson if routing, bootstrap, review, or
         integration behaviour should affect future swarm runs.
    category: quality
